.TH CHGRP 1.SH NAMEchgrp \- set the group of a file's owner.SH SYNOPSIS\fBchgrp\fR[\-z] group file ....SH DESCRIPTIONAn OS-9 file's owner is a group.user pair.\fBchgrp\fRsets the group half of it for each file named and leaves the userhalf alone:\fBchgrp 5 notes\fRturns an owner of 0.0 into 5.0.  Use\fBdir \-e\fRto see the owner..PPThe group may be a number or a user name from\fB/dd/SYS/password\fR.\fROnly the number form works with the password file this disk ships,whose entries read\fBtester,,1.7,...\fR:\fRgiven a name,\fBchgrp\fRanswers\fBUid field for tester in password file is invalid!\fR.\fR.SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-z\fRread the file names from the standard input, one to a line..SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fRChanging the owner of a file you do not own needs the super user..SH SEE ALSOchown, attr, dir